# Environment for the PUBLIC /v1 process — a different process from the
# dashboard API, with a different config file.
#
# Copy to `server/.env.public` (gitignored) and edit. Two commands read it:
#
#   npm run dev:public     the public /v1 server  (src/v1/publicIndex.ts)
#   npm run keys -- ...    the API-key CLI        (src/v1/keys/keysCli.ts)
#
# `server/.env` is the PRIVATE app's file and is NOT read here. That separation
# is the point: the private app's environment carries ENERGY_DB_PATH and
# HELIO_WRITE_TOKEN, and the public composition refuses to start if it is handed
# either kind of capability (FORBIDDEN_PUBLIC_ENV, src/v1/publicEnv.ts).

# --- API key store (ABL-300) ----------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED, and deliberately with no default.
#
# This is its OWN SQLite file. It must never be the shared energy database:
# that file is 376 GiB, is owned by the `energy-data-gathering` sibling module,
# and is opened readonly by this server — writing account and key rows into it
# would add a write path contending with ingest, in a schema we do not own.
# `resolveApiKeysDbPath` refuses to start if this path and ENERGY_DB_PATH
# resolve to the same file, and refuses the `/data/energy_dashboard.db` default
# too.
#
# There is no default because a credentials file must not land somewhere nobody
# chose. The serving process opens this readonly and requires it to exist; the
# keys CLI opens it read-write and creates it.
#
#   cd server
#   npm run keys -- accounts:create --name "Acme Energy" --plan developer
#   npm run keys -- keys:issue --account acct_... --label "prod ETL"
#
# The key is printed once and cannot be recovered: the store holds
# sha256(secret) plus the non-secret 8-character prefix. A lost key is rotated,
# never re-read.
API_KEYS_DB_PATH=C:/Code/able/data/api_keys.db

# --- The change log (ABL-532) ----------------------------------------------
# `changelog_entries` lives in the SAME file as the key store, above. No second
# path to set, for the reason the usage tables give below: one file, one guard,
# and `resolveApiKeysDbPath` is the only thing that decides where it is.
#
# It is a table rather than a set of committed files because PUBLISH LATENCY IS
# CONTRACTUAL. A change that corrects wrong values may be served immediately, and
# its change-log entry has to go up at the same time. This repository has no
# CI/CD and is deployed by hand, so an entry that needed a build would go up
# hours or days after the change it announces. Publishing is:
#
#   cd server
#   npm run changelog -- entries:publish --type correction \
#       --effective 2026-08-22T14:00:00Z --title "..." --detail "..." \
#       --what-was-wrong "..."
#
# and the serving process picks it up on the next request. Nothing to rebuild,
# nothing to restart. The serving process opens the table READONLY; the CLI holds
# the only read-write handle, exactly as with keys.
#
# The publication instant is stamped at insert and cannot be supplied — a 30-day
# notice must not be manufacturable after the fact — and there is no command that
# edits or deletes a published entry. If one is wrong, publish another.
#
# A store with no `changelog_entries` table makes the serving process REFUSE TO
# START rather than serve an empty change log at a URL a subscriber is pointed
# at. Create it with:
#
#   npm run changelog -- entries:init
#
# That creates the table, publishes nothing, and is safe to re-run. An EMPTY
# change log is fine — it serves an empty page; only a MISSING table refuses.
#
# `entries:seed --examples` is a separate command and is NOT how you create the
# store. It publishes two entries that describe no real change (they render with
# a marker saying so), and there is no command that deletes one. Never run it
# against a store that serves real subscribers.
#
# The page is at /changelog and /changelog.json, deliberately OUTSIDE /v1: we
# commit to announcing the retirement of a major API version through it, and a
# change log under /v1 would be withdrawn by the very event it announces. It is
# unauthenticated, and it loads no stylesheet, script, font or image at all.

# --- Usage metering and retention (ABL-301) --------------------------------
# The usage tables (`usage_events`, `usage_rollup`) live in the SAME file as the
# key store, above. There is no second path to set, deliberately: one file, one
# guard, and `resolveApiKeysDbPath` is the only thing that decides where it is.
#
# Metering is always on. It is not switchable, because an API that serves paid
# traffic and bills nobody is not a mode anybody wants to be one variable away
# from — see `usageMeter` in `src/v1/publicApp.ts`.
#
# The two periods below are PUBLISHED COMMITMENTS from the ABL-297 privacy
# notice §5, not tuning knobs. They are read from configuration because counsel
# may change the numbers and that must be a config change rather than a
# migration — but changing one here changes a statement we make to a paying
# subscriber, so it is a decision to take with the notice open, not a default to
# adjust. Both are also printed by `npm run usage -- usage:stats`, which reports
# NOT COMPLIANT if any record past the first boundary still holds personal data.
#
#   USAGE_PII_RETENTION_DAYS      the source IP and user agent are cleared from
#                                 a request record at this age.   Published: 90
#   USAGE_EVENT_RETENTION_MONTHS  the de-identified record is deleted at this
#                                 age, in calendar months.        Published: 13
#
# The monthly aggregates in `usage_rollup` are NOT covered by either and are
# never deleted by anything in this codebase: accounting law requires the
# figures an invoice was based on to be kept for ~7 years, which is why they are
# a separate materialised record instead of a query over the raw log.
# USAGE_PII_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# USAGE_EVENT_RETENTION_MONTHS=13
#
# How long after a month ends before it may be closed. A closed month is final
# and is never recomputed, so this is the margin for a maintenance pass that did
# not run on time.
# USAGE_MONTH_CLOSE_GRACE_DAYS=2

# --- Billing, TEST MODE ONLY (ABL-307) -------------------------------------
# None of these is read by the serving process. `publicAppGraph.test.ts`
# asserts that nothing under `v1/billing/` is reachable from either entrypoint:
# a subscription is never consulted to serve a request, so billing lives
# entirely in `npm run billing -- <command>`, beside `npm run keys` and
# `npm run usage`.
#
#   BILLING_PRICE_BOOK_PATH   what each plan costs, as a JSON file.
#
# UNSET IS THE CORRECT SETTING TODAY. Board Decision 1 (tier structure and
# price points) is open, so no price list is committed to this repository and
# there are no defaults to fall back to. Unset, `billing:price-book` prints the
# shape to fill in and names the open decision; metering, subscription state
# and `billing:reconcile`'s request figures all work without it, and only the
# amounts on an invoice need it.
#
# When the Board rules, the file is validated on load against
# `quota/planLimits.ts` and REFUSED if the two disagree — the gate serves
# against one set of numbers and the invoice charges against the other, so a
# price change has to move both in the same reviewed diff.
# BILLING_PRICE_BOOK_PATH=/etc/able/price-book.json
#
#   BILLING_SUPPLIER_COUNTRY  where Able Energy is established, ISO-3166-1
#                             alpha-2. REQUIRED for an invoice, no default.
#
# Every EU VAT decision turns on it — domestic rate, reverse charge, or the
# destination member state's rate — and a guessed country puts a confidently
# wrong rate on every invoice, so `resolveSupplier` refuses rather than
# assuming one.
# BILLING_SUPPLIER_COUNTRY=DE
# BILLING_SUPPLIER_VAT_ID=DE999999999
#
# Whether we can declare destination-country VAT on a single return. It does
# not change the rate; it changes whether there is a return to put it on, and
# an invoice that needs one we do not have is blocked rather than issued.
# BILLING_SUPPLIER_OSS_REGISTERED=false
#
# The €10,000 union-wide threshold election: below it, a supplier may keep
# charging its domestic rate on cross-border B2C supplies. An election, not a
# default — false (destination rates) is correct at any turnover.
# BILLING_SUPPLIER_BELOW_UNION_THRESHOLD=false
#
#   BILLING_PROVIDER_SECRET   a payment-provider credential.
#
# MUST STAY UNSET. There is no network client in this codebase to use one with,
# and ABL-307 is test mode only: no live keys, no real payments, no customer
# data until the Board approves launch and ABL-349's legal review of the
# subscriber terms is complete. If it is set at all, `assertTestModeSecret`
# refuses anything that is not a provider TEST key and refuses any credential
# it cannot classify — a secret it cannot classify is one it cannot promise is
# not live.
# BILLING_PROVIDER_SECRET=

# --- Energy data (ABL-303) -------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED, and deliberately with no default.
#
# The readonly energy database the dashboard also reads. `config/database.ts`
# falls back to `/data/energy_dashboard.db` when this is unset; the public
# process does NOT, because a fallback means a misconfigured public API opens
# *something* — or fails on the first paying customer's request rather than at
# startup. It is opened readonly, with `fileMustExist`, before the port is
# bound, so a path typo is a startup failure and never an API that answers
# "we hold nothing for that zone" for every zone on earth.
#
# `resolveEnergyDbPath` reuses `resolveApiKeysDbPath` and refuses to start if
# this and API_KEYS_DB_PATH resolve to the same file — the mirror of the guard
# above, so the two databases cannot be confused from either direction.
ENERGY_DB_PATH=C:/Code/able/data/energy_dashboard.db

# --- Public base URL (ABL-303) ---------------------------------------------
# The origin subscribers reach this API on, used to build `links.next` for
# pagination. OPTIONAL, and UNSET IS THE CORRECT SETTING ON THE LAN.
#
# Unset, pagination links come back RELATIVE (`/v1/observations/load?...`),
# which is correct against whatever origin the client already used. Set, they
# are absolute against this value.
#
# What must never happen — and the reason this is configuration at all — is a
# link built from the request host (ABL-291 brief §2). The framework-tutorial
# version of that line works perfectly here and bakes
# `http://192.168.86.36:3002/...` into a subscriber's stored URLs, discovered
# only after the API moves. There is no code path under `v1/` that can read a
# host off a request; `links.test.ts` asserts that by scanning the sources.
#
# A path prefix is allowed (a gateway may mount us under one). A query string
# or fragment is refused at startup, because pagination appends its own query
# and either would be silently discarded.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com

# --- Binding ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults to 127.0.0.1:3002. PUBLIC_BIND_HOST exists so the bind address is
# configuration rather than a code change — but setting it to anything other
# than loopback is a network-exposure decision that needs its own
# Board-approved issue (ABL-291). This process is not deployed or exposed.
# PUBLIC_PORT=3002
# PUBLIC_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1

# --- CORS ------------------------------------------------------------------
# A comma-separated allowlist of browser origins, defaulting to EMPTY = deny.
# Unlike the private app (`origin: true` + `credentials: true`), nothing is
# reflected back. Non-browser callers — curl, a server-side SDK — are
# unaffected either way; CORS is a browser control, never a server-side one.
# PUBLIC_CORS_ORIGINS=https://docs.example.com,https://app.example.com

# --- What must NOT be in this file -----------------------------------------
# HELIO_WRITE_TOKEN, JAO_CORE_NET_POSITION_ENABLED, OPS_PEER_URL, COMMIT_SHA.
# `createPublicApp` throws at startup if any is set, naming the variable and
# never its value. Give them to the private app instead — it is a separate
# process with its own `.env`.
#
# ENERGY_DB_PATH is not forbidden (the key-store guard reads it to check that
# the two paths differ), but nothing in the public graph opens the energy
# database: `publicAppGraph.test.ts` asserts `config/` is unreachable from both
# entrypoints.
